@page "/workout"

<PageTitle>Full workout</PageTitle>
<h1>Full workout</h1>
<p>The kitchen-sink page: one registration form that exercises the library's features side by side. The details section pairs an async availability check with a cross-field date rule and a coupon only the server can judge; the catering section is gated by pure UI state while its rule stays unconditional. Two ways out: <em>Save draft</em> runs the Draft profile, and <em>Submit registration</em> runs the Submit profile and then posts to the API.</p>
@if (IsHostedDemo)
{
    <p><small>This hosted demo has no server behind it — an in-browser handler answers with the same validators and the same response shapes the real API would send, coupon rejection included.</small></p>
}

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Workout.razor", "Workout.razor.cs", "EventRegistration.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Contact email</strong> — required at submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Contact email</strong> — checked for a valid address, validated live.</li>
            <li><strong>Contact email</strong> — checked asynchronously as you type for availability (a fixed 300&nbsp;ms delay stands in for the server call). <code>taken@@example.com</code> is already registered.</li>
            <li><strong>Event name</strong> — required at submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Event date</strong> — required at submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Event date</strong> must be a real date, validated live.</li>
            <li><strong>Early-bird deadline</strong> must be a real date, validated live.</li>
            <li>The <strong>early-bird deadline</strong> must fall on or before the <strong>event date</strong> — a cross-field rule that runs only once both parse.</li>
            <li>Both date fields set <code>UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnBlur"</code>: a native date input fires <code>change</code> per segment as you type, so the mode commits the value on every one of those but waits for the field to lose focus before notifying the engine, once the value has actually settled.</li>
            <li>Draft and Submit stay orthogonal: malformed-value rules (formats, ranges, the availability check) run always, presence rules only at submit — with <strong>Dietary notes</strong> as the deliberate exception, a presence rule left in the always-on bucket precisely so the UI alone gates it (see below). <em>Save draft</em> asks the validator for the Draft profile directly, so it answers about that bucket and no other; <em>Submit registration</em> runs the form's submit pipeline.</li>
            <li><strong>Coupon code</strong> — no client rule at all: the server owns the code list, and its 400 lands on this field through <code>ApplyServerIssues</code>. Each response replaces the previous server verdict, so a corrected resubmission never leaves a stale coupon error behind.</li>
            <li>Before posting, the page calls <code>Normalize()</code>, which trims <strong>Contact email</strong>.</li>
            <li><code>Normalize()</code> also trims <strong>Description</strong>.</li>
            <li><code>Normalize()</code> collapses <strong>Event name</strong>'s internal whitespace to single spaces.</li>
            <li><code>Normalize()</code> trims and upper-cases <strong>Coupon code</strong>, so <code>welcome10</code> and the server's <code>WELCOME10</code> are the same code.</li>
            <li><strong>Catering headcount</strong> — a whole number between 1 and 500, checked only while catering is included.</li>
            <li><strong>Dietary notes</strong> — required, and deliberately unconditional. The checkbox gates the field's visibility, never the rule: hide the field and the rule still fails, so the submit blocks with a form-level explanation rather than with a message you cannot see.</li>
            <li>Editing after a submit schedules a background refresh that reconciles the whole form, while the pending indicator stays on the field you edited.</li>
            <li><strong>Attendees</strong> — add and remove rows freely.</li>
            <li>Each row's <strong>Name</strong> is required, checked only at submit, like the presence rules above.</li>
            <li>Each row's <strong>Email</strong>, when given, must be a valid address, checked only at submit, like the presence rules above.</li>
            <li>The attendee list itself carries a collection-level rule from the always-on bucket: more than 10 attendees draws a warning ("More than 10 attendees needs approval — submission is not blocked"), updating the moment a row is added or removed rather than waiting for submit.</li>
            <li>The attendee list also carries a collection-level rule for having none at all: it draws an info ("You can add attendees now or after registering"), updating the same way. Both are advisories, unlike the error-severity rules above — they never block submission.</li>
            <li><strong>Sessions</strong> — 150 rows in a virtualized panel. Each row's <strong>Seats</strong> must be a whole number between 0 and 500, and that rule sits in the always-on bucket, so a row answers the moment you edit it rather than waiting for submit.</li>
            <li>Disclosure is render-registration, and virtualized rows come and go: the seats input sets <code>KeepRegistered</code>, which keeps a scrolled-past row's registration alive after it leaves the window. This page then goes further and sets a <code>DisclosureOverride</code> for the collection — an override answers before the registry is consulted, so every session row is disclosed, including rows Virtualize has never rendered. Validation always runs against the full in-memory model; only <em>visibility</em> is render-gated, so the override costs nothing.</li>
            <li>Clicking the summary entry of a row that is not in the DOM uses the summary's <code>FocusFallback</code>: the page scrolls the panel, lets Virtualize render the row, and the summary retries the focus once.</li>
            <li><strong>Ticket tier</strong> — a foreign control: a plain <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> this page renders itself. The renderless <code>FormidableField</code> hands it everything a wrapped input gets for nothing — element id, state class, aria attributes, and <code>NotifyChanged()</code> — so it styles, carries messages and takes focus from the summary like the rest of the form. Its presence rule belongs to Submit, and the form opens on <em>General admission</em>, so it only speaks up if you choose the blank option.</li>
            <li><strong>Venue region</strong> — the same seam from the other side: a native <code>InputText</code> with a native <code>ValidationMessage</code>, inside a Formidable form. Its presence rule belongs to Submit like the ones above, and the wiring is what carries it: the engine writes error-severity issues into the EditContext's message store, so the native <code>ValidationMessage</code> speaks the verdict ("Venue region is required") without a Formidable component in sight; the engine installs a css class provider on that same EditContext, so native <code>InputBase</code> components pick up the same state classes; and a <code>FormidableFieldAnchor</code> registers a natively-rendered field so its issues disclose instead of being suppressed as unrevealed. The one thing the shared EditContext cannot carry is click-to-focus: it addresses a field by the element id Formidable's own inputs render, and a native input renders none — so this page renders it, giving the input the field's id and the native <code>ValidationMessage</code> the matching messages id the input names in its <code>aria-describedby</code>. The other thing a field context supplies and a native input cannot ask for is <code>aria-invalid</code>, so the page reads that from the engine and renders it too — absent while the field is clean, <code>"true"</code> once it has an error. One addition per concern is what makes the crossings the shared context cannot: <code>FormidableFieldAnchor</code> for disclosure, the id for focus, <code>aria-describedby</code> and <code>aria-invalid</code> for the screen reader.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            @if (IsHostedDemo)
            {
                <li>No server to start on this hosted demo — the in-browser handler answers as soon as you submit, coupon rejection included.</li>
            }
            else
            {
                <li>Start the API first: <code>dotnet run --project samples/Formidable.Sample.Api</code>.</li>
            }
            <li>Click <em>Submit registration</em> with everything empty — every presence rule whose field is still empty answers at once, the summary lists them, and every entry has somewhere to land: an entry that names an input focuses that input, native ones included (step 15), and the attendee advisory focuses the Attendees group, which owns no input of its own. A group or the form scrolls into view and takes focus quietly under the mouse; reach the same entry from the keyboard and the landing shows an accent outline as well. The form-level entry the gate raises lands on the form itself (step 5).</li>
            <li>Type <code>taken@@example.com</code> into Contact email — "checking…" sits on that field for the 300 ms the availability check takes, then the taken message lands.</li>
            <li>Replace Contact email with a fresh address (the one from the last step is already taken), fill in Event name, Event date, Dietary notes and <strong>Venue region</strong>, and submit with the coupon <code>BOGUS</code>. The server can only speak once the client is clean, and the POST happens only after a valid client submit — so the coupon's verdict always arrives last: the server's 400 lands inline on Coupon code. Change it to <code>WELCOME10</code> and submit again: the new verdict replaces the old one and the registration is accepted.</li>
            <li>With everything else valid, clear Dietary notes and submit — blocked, inline and in the summary. Now untick <em>Include catering</em> (the field and its inline message leave the form — the summary entry waits for the next submit) and submit again: with nothing on screen able to carry that issue, the form blocks with "information that is not currently displayed is invalid" instead. The rule never relented — only its visibility changed.</li>
            <li>Put <code>nope@@</code> in Contact email and click <em>Save draft</em> — the draft answers about the always-on bucket: the malformed address, plus Dietary notes if you emptied it. Event name and Event date stay silent, because their presence rules belong to Submit.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Add attendee</em> — a blank row appears with Name and Email fields. Leave Name empty and click <em>Submit registration</em> — the row's own message names it ("Attendee name is required") and the summary lists it too; clicking the summary entry focuses that row's Name field.</li>
            <li>Fill that row's Name, then click <em>Add attendee</em> ten more times, giving each a name (Email can stay blank) — once the count passes ten, the warning "More than 10 attendees needs approval — submission is not blocked" appears above the list.</li>
            <li>Put the rest of the form back in the state that submitted successfully earlier (a real Contact email, catering ticked with a dietary note filled in, <strong>Venue region</strong> still holding a value) and submit again — the warning does not stop it: the registration is accepted with 11 attendees (the response body echoes the count).</li>
            <li>Click <em>Remove</em> on every row until none remain — the warning gives way to the info "You can add attendees now or after registering".</li>
            <li>Scroll the session panel to the bottom, set the last session's <strong>Seats</strong> to 900 and press Tab so the edit commits — the row objects on the spot — then scroll back to the top and click <em>Submit registration</em> — the submit blocks and the summary carries "Seats must be a whole number between 0 and 500" for a row that is nowhere on screen. The <code>DisclosureOverride</code> is what lets it speak for that row: no registration is consulted, so the summary would report it even had you never scrolled that far.</li>
            <li>Click that summary entry — the panel scrolls itself, Virtualize renders the row, and focus lands in its Seats box. That is the fallback: click-to-focus reaches rendered elements only, so the page makes the row renderable and the summary tries again. Put the row back to <code>0</code> and the refresh that follows any post-submit edit takes both the inline message and the summary entry away.</li>
            <li>Set <strong>Ticket tier</strong> to the blank <em>Choose…</em> option and submit — the foreign select takes the same red border, the same inline message and the same summary entry as any wrapped input on this page, and clicking that entry moves focus into the select. The page renders the control; <code>FormidableField</code> supplies everything the wrapper would have — then pick a tier again so later steps start clean.</li>
            <li>Type a region into <strong>Venue region</strong> and tab out — the native input takes on the same "confirmed" border a Formidable input shows once it has been edited and has nothing to complain about. Nothing was wired per field to do that: the css class provider serves both kinds of input.</li>
            <li>Now clear <strong>Venue region</strong> and submit — the native <code>ValidationMessage</code> shows "Venue region is required": Formidable's verdict, rendered by a component that knows nothing about Formidable. Click that summary entry and focus lands in the native input, exactly as it does for the wrapped fields above: the page hands that input the same id a Formidable input would have rendered, and the id is the whole of what the focus service looks for. Inspect the input while the error stands and it also carries <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> and the <code>aria-describedby</code> naming the message below it — the page renders both, because a native input has no field context to take them from. State classes, messages, disclosure, focus and aria all cross the seam here: the first two ride the EditContext both kinds of input share, and the rest the page renders itself — the anchor, the id, and the two aria attributes.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_registration" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="SendToServer" OnInvalidSubmit="HandleBlocked">
    <FormidableSummary FocusFallback="ScrollToSessionAsync" />

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Registration details</legend>

        <FormidableField For="() => _registration.ContactEmail" Context="field">
            <div class="field">
                <label>Contact email <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.ContactEmail" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
                @if (field.State.IsValidating)
                {
                    <em role="status">checking…</em>
                }
            </div>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.ContactEmail" />
        </FormidableField>

        <div class="field"><label>Event name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.EventName" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.EventName" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Event date <FormidableInputText type="date" @bind-Value="_registration.EventDate" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnBlur" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.EventDate" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Early-bird deadline <FormidableInputText type="date" @bind-Value="_registration.EarlyBirdDeadline" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnBlur" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.EarlyBirdDeadline" /></div>

        <div class="field"><label>Description <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.Description" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.Description" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Coupon code <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.CouponCode" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.CouponCode" /></div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Catering</legend>

        <FormidableField For="() => _registration.IncludeCatering" Context="field">
            <label><input type="checkbox" id="@field.ElementId"
                          checked="@_registration.IncludeCatering"
                          @onchange="args => ToggleCatering(args, field)" /> Include catering</label>
        </FormidableField>

        <p><small>Unticking hides the catering fields but not their rules: <strong>Dietary notes</strong> is required whether or not you can see it, so a hidden empty note blocks the submit with a form-level message rather than with one you cannot reach.</small></p>

        @if (_registration.IncludeCatering)
        {
            <div class="field"><label>Catering headcount <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.CateringHeadcount" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.CateringHeadcount" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Dietary notes <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.DietaryNotes" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.DietaryNotes" /></div>
        }
    </fieldset>

    <FormidableField For="() => _registration.Attendees" Context="field">
        <fieldset id="@field.ElementId" tabindex="-1">
            <legend>Attendees</legend>

            <FormidableCollectionMessage For="() => _registration.Attendees" />

            <ul class="member-list">
                @foreach (var attendee in _registration.Attendees)
                {
                    <li class="field" @key="attendee">
                        <label>Name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="attendee.Name" /></label>
                        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => attendee.Name" />
                        <label>Email <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="attendee.Email" /></label>
                        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => attendee.Email" />
                        <div class="actions">
                            <button type="button" @onclick="() => RemoveAttendee(attendee, field)">Remove</button>
                        </div>
                    </li>
                }
            </ul>

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="button" @onclick="() => AddAttendee(field)">Add attendee</button>
            </div>
        </fieldset>
    </FormidableField>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Sessions</legend>

        <p><small>150 sessions in a virtualized panel: only the rows near the scroll position exist in the DOM at all, so their inputs come and go as you scroll. The wiring below keeps their issues on the books regardless.</small></p>

        <div class="scroll-panel">
            <Virtualize Items="_registration.Sessions" ItemSize="SessionRowHeight" Context="session">
                <div class="field" @key="session">
                    <label>@session.Title
                        <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="session.Seats" KeepRegistered="true" />
                    </label>
                    <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => session.Seats" />
                </div>
            </Virtualize>
        </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Ticket and venue</legend>

        <FormidableField For="() => _registration.TicketTier" Context="field">
            <div class="field">
                <label for="@field.ElementId">Ticket tier (foreign select)</label>
                <select @attributes="field.InputAttributes"
                        value="@_registration.TicketTier" @onchange="args => ChangeTicketTier(args, field)">
                    <option value="">Choose…</option>
                    <option>General admission</option>
                    <option>VIP</option>
                    <option>Speaker</option>
                </select>
            </div>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.TicketTier" />
        </FormidableField>

        <div class="field"><label>Venue region (native InputText) <InputText @bind-Value="_registration.VenueRegion" id="@VenueRegionId" aria-invalid="@VenueRegionAriaInvalid" aria-describedby="@VenueRegionMessagesId" /></label>
            <ValidationMessage For="() => _registration.VenueRegion" id="@VenueRegionMessagesId" />
            <FormidableFieldAnchor For="() => _registration.VenueRegion" /></div>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="button" @onclick="SaveDraft">Save draft</button>
        <button type="submit" class="primary">Submit registration</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
